## A deliberately small initial stable set

`Deinitable`, `Movable`, `Copyable`, `Array`, `List`, `Span`, `String`, `Bool`, `Optional`.

{#0F766E}1.0 is a promise about *these*, not about the whole library. Everything else may still move.{/}

## Per-API stability markings in the reference

Stability is recorded on the API itself rather than inferred from the module it lives in.

{#0F766E}Check the marking before you depend on something in a library you publish — the 1.0 label on the language does not extend to every symbol.{/}

## A deprecated alias plus a compiler fix-it

Nearly every breaking change in 1.0 ships both, so old code keeps compiling and the compiler tells you what the new spelling is.

{#0F766E}Compile, then apply the fix-its. Most of the migration is mechanical.{/}

## Because deprecated aliases are *designed* to keep compiling

{#C62828}A file full of beta spellings builds clean.{/} Silence from the compiler means the aliases are working, not that you have migrated.

{#0F766E}Treat deprecation warnings as the real work list.{/}

## One name, and one type, per concept

The beta library had several spellings for the same idea and several types doing one job.

{#0F766E}It explains the whole rename set at once: `StringSpan`, `Array`, `Deinitable`, the unified `Pointer`, and `size` becoming `length`.{/}

## `Pointer` and `UnsafePointer` unifying

{#C62828}Two types for one concept became one.{/} Safety moved onto the operations, which are prefixed `unsafe_`.

{#0F766E}The old distinction is gone: it implied a pointer was safe or unsafe as a whole, when in fact individual operations are.{/}

## `ImplicitlyDestructible` becoming `Deinitable`

It appears in nearly every generic bound in beta-era library code, alongside the `__del__` to `__deinit__` change it accompanies.

{#C62828}Both ship deprecated aliases, so neither breaks the build.{/}

## `InlineArray` becoming `Array`

{#C62828}Renamed *and* narrowed.{/} Its parameters changed — `ElementType` to `T`, `size` to `length` — and it is no longer `ImplicitlyCopyable` or `Defaultable`.

{#0F766E}The fix-it renames the type. It cannot fix the copies you were getting for free.{/}

## `imm` is the preferred spelling

{#C62828}`read` is deprecated{/} for both the argument convention and closure capture.

{#0F766E}The hardest rename to grep for: `read` is also an ordinary English word and an ordinary method name.{/}

## `size` becoming `length`

It was applied across the library, not just to `SIMD` — `List.resize(new_length=)`, `ComplexSIMD[..., length=N]`, `Array`'s parameter.

{#C62828}`size` remains a legitimate identifier elsewhere, so a plain search over it is mostly noise.{/}

## `ImplicitlyCopyable`

An error value can be copied without an explicit request, which is what makes propagating one through `raise` and `except` unremarkable.

{#0F766E}Errors are values in Mojo, and this is the conformance that makes them behave like the small values they are.{/}

## The renames, not your logic

Most 1.0 breakage is one of: a rename with a fix-it, `Array` losing implicit copyability, a `where` clause moving to trailing position, or `alias` becoming `comptime`.

{#0F766E}Semantics changed in few places; spellings changed in many.{/}

## A full container set

`Set`, `Optional`, `String`, `Array`, `Span`, `Deque`, `BitSet`, `BinaryHeap`, `LinkedList`, `Counter`, `Interval`, `TypeDict`.

{#0F766E}`BitSet` is a compact set of non-negative integers in inline storage; `Interval` is a balanced tree for interval queries.{/}

## Fixed size at compile time versus dynamic

`Array` is fixed-size with compile-time length checking; `List` grows.

{#0F766E}A list expression like `[1, 2, 3]` now builds an `Array`, so reaching for `List` is a decision you make, not a default you get.{/}

## Only when its element type is

Several collections conform conditionally, which is what lets them hold unique resources that must not be silently dropped.

{#0F766E}The container inherits the discipline of what it contains.{/}

## `deinit_with()`

You supply the operation to run on each element, so a container of explicitly destroyed values can be torn down without the container knowing how.

{#0F766E}The reason a `List` of file handles is expressible at all.{/}

## Yes — `Array` supports non-`Movable` elements

The element is constructed in place rather than moved in, so a pinned type can live in a container.

{#0F766E}Before this, opting out of `Movable` meant opting out of collections.{/}

## In-place construction with `init_with=`

The constructor builds the payload where it will live instead of moving it in.

{#0F766E}Same trick as the containers: if you cannot move a value, construct it at its final address.{/}

## No — it is `Iterable`

{#C62828}It is not an `Iterator`.{/} It can be iterated, yielding zero or one item, but it does not itself carry iteration state.

{#0F766E}The distinction matters when a generic bound asks for one specifically.{/}

## Owned iteration no longer requires `Copyable`

Consuming a container yields its elements by ownership, so elements that cannot be copied can still be iterated out.

{#0F766E}Iterating to move values out of a container is now ordinary.{/}

## Consuming iteration is the one that can yield linear values

Borrowed iteration hands out references and leaves the dictionary intact; consuming iteration takes the entries out.

{#0F766E}Which one you get follows from how you pass the dictionary.{/}

## Insertion and clearing

Inserting over an existing key, and clearing the container, now handle elements that must be explicitly destroyed rather than dropping them.

{#C62828}A silently discarded linear value is exactly the bug the type system exists to prevent.{/}

## `Tuple.consume_elements()`

It moves the elements out rather than copying them, which is what a tuple of unique resources requires.

{#0F766E}The tuple is consumed — that is the point, not a side effect.{/}

## The dictionary behind keyword arguments

{#C62828}Renamed from `OwnedKwargsDict`.{/} It maps string keys to owned values, and is what `var **kwargs` gives you.

{#0F766E}The new name says what it is rather than where it came from.{/}

## Prelude aliases for the two `Span` mutabilities

They save spelling out the origin parameter for the common cases, and are available without an import.

{#0F766E}`Span` also carries an address-space parameter, so a GPU shared-memory span is a distinct type from a host one.{/}

## A borrowed view of string data

{#C62828}Renamed from `StringSlice`.{/} It refers to text it does not own, carrying the origin of whatever does.

{#0F766E}Take a `StringSpan` in a function that only reads text — it accepts literals and owned strings alike without copying.{/}

## `Python.import_module()`

```
var np = Python.import_module("numpy")
var array = np.array(Python.list(1, 2, 3))
```

It returns the module as a `PythonObject`, which you must keep in a variable to reach its members.

## No — modules only

There is no equivalent of `from x import y`. Import the module and go through it.

{#0F766E}Python built-ins are not automatic either: import the `builtins` module to reach them.{/}

## Inside a function — not at top level

{#C62828}Mojo has no top-level code, so the import lives in a method.{/}

It may also raise, so the caller must handle or propagate the error.

## A Mojo wrapper around any Python value

Modules, classes, instances and results all arrive as one. Attribute access and calls on it dispatch into CPython.

{#0F766E}One Mojo type stands in for the entire Python type system.{/}

## Through CPython's abstract protocols

Arithmetic, comparison and membership dispatch through the protocol slots instead of a slower generic path — roughly a twelvefold speedup.

{#0F766E}The behavior also matches CPython's own more exactly, which was the more important half.{/}

## Copy helpers, or a zero-copy borrow

The borrowing form gives Mojo a view over the array's existing buffer.

{#C62828}A borrowed view is only valid while the Python object lives.{/} {#0F766E}Copy when the lifetime is unclear; borrow when the array is large and you control its lifetime.{/}

## It becomes a Mojo error

The call site handles it with ordinary `try`/`except`.

{#0F766E}This is why so much of the Python interop surface is marked `raises`.{/}

## `PythonModuleBuilder`

```
var m = PythonModuleBuilder("mojo_module")
m.def_function[factorial](
    "factorial", docstring="Compute n!"
)
return m.finalize()
```

`finalize()` produces the module object.

## No — only the `PyInit_` entry point does

You pass the function by reference to `def_function`, and Mojo generates the C wrapper CPython actually calls.

{#0F766E}You write ordinary Mojo functions; the binding layer is generated.{/}

## C has no exceptions to propagate into

{#C62828}An unhandled error at that boundary has nowhere to go.{/} Catch it inside and call `abort()`, or translate it into a Python exception.

{#0F766E}The constraint applies to the entry point, not to the functions it registers.{/}

## A `@staticmethod` taking the receiver explicitly

```
@staticmethod
def get_name(py_self: PythonObject) raises -> PythonObject:
    var self_ptr = py_self.downcast_value_ptr[Self]()
    return self_ptr[].name
```

The receiver arrives as `py_self: PythonObject` or `self_ptr: Pointer[Self]`.

## `downcast_value_ptr[Type]()`, or `ConvertibleFromPython`

The downcast gives a pointer to the Mojo value inside a bound Python object; the trait converts an ordinary Python value into a Mojo one.

{#0F766E}Downcast for your own bound types; convert for Python's.{/}

## Safe `Pointer` types

{#C62828}The binding APIs moved off raw unsafe pointers.{/} A callback receives `Pointer[Self]` rather than an untyped address.

{#0F766E}The boundary is still unsafe in principle; the signatures no longer pretend otherwise by being vague.{/}

## `check_symbol()` before `get_function()`

```
if lib.check_symbol("exp10"):
    var exp10 = lib.get_function[c_double]("exp10")
```

The pattern for optional platform features — asking is cheaper than crashing.

## The unversioned name belongs to the development package

It is a symlink that may be absent at run time, and may point at a different ABI.

{#0F766E}Load the ABI-versioned name — it is the one that encodes the contract you compiled against.{/}

## Starting the Mojo runtime when a foreign host calls in

From `std.runtime.asyncrt`, it initializes the global runtime if it is not already running.

{#0F766E}Needed when Mojo is a shared library being called by C or Python — nothing else has started the runtime for you.{/}

## Carrying an address whose type C does not tell you

It is `Pointer[NoneType]` — the `void *` of Mojo — and appears in callback signatures such as a `qsort` comparator.

{#0F766E}Recover the type with `unsafe_bitcast`, which is where you take responsibility for being right.{/}

## Architecture, CPU model, and feature set

x86-64 or AArch64; a specific processor; and the instruction extensions such as AVX-512 or Neon.

{#0F766E}For GPU work an accelerator architecture is added on top — NVIDIA, AMD or Apple Metal.{/}

## One changes the instructions, the other only the scheduling

`--mcpu` selects a CPU and may emit instructions only it has. `--mtune` optimizes for a CPU without changing which instructions are used.

{#0F766E}`--mtune` keeps the binary portable; `--mcpu` does not.{/}

## That path is what makes it auto-discoverable

The compiler looks there, so a package installed to the right place needs no path configuration.

{#0F766E}Output it somewhere else and the import will not resolve, even though the build succeeded.{/}

## It is tied to the compiler version that produced it

{#C62828}Precompiled files may not be compatible with other compiler versions.{/}

{#0F766E}Which is why package recipes pin the compiler version strictly.{/}

## As a conda package, built with rattler-build

Write a `recipe.yaml`, build it with `rattler-build`, then upload the `.conda` file to a channel.

{#0F766E}The modular-community channel on prefix.dev is the shared destination.{/}

## A module-level `def` whose name starts with `test_`

It takes no arguments, returns `None`, and signals failure by raising.

{#C62828}It must be at module scope — a struct method will not be discovered.{/}

## The usual set, plus a tolerance form

`assert_true`, `assert_false`, `assert_equal`, `assert_not_equal`, `assert_almost_equal` (with `atol` and `rtol`), and `assert_raises`.

{#0F766E}All take an optional `msg`.{/}

## `--only`, `--skip`, `--skip-all`

`--only` runs the named tests, `--skip` excludes them, and `--skip-all` lists tests without running any.

{#0F766E}You can also skip programmatically with `suite.skip[test_name]()` before `suite^.run()`.{/}

## Packaged instructions that teach an AI assistant current Mojo

They ship with the toolchain so a coding assistant works from 1.0 conventions rather than from beta-era code it was trained on.

{#0F766E}Directly relevant here: Mojo changed enough in 1.0 that plausible-looking generated code is often beta code.{/}

## A curated numeric surface

Trigonometric, exponential, logarithmic and special functions, plus constants.

{#0F766E}Curated, not exhaustive — the functions operate on SIMD types, so they vectorize rather than looping element by element.{/}

## The OS interface layer

Environment variables, the filesystem, and process control.

{#0F766E}Paths and file operations live here; the lower-level hardware and intrinsic surface is `std.sys`.{/}

## System runtime and hardware facts

I/O, hardware information, intrinsics, and compile-time utilities — including `platform_map` and the accelerator queries used by `comptime if`.

{#0F766E}This is where code asks what machine it is being compiled for.{/}

## Whenever the answer matters

It runs enough iterations to be statistically meaningful and reports the analysis, rather than handing you one sample.

{#C62828}A single timed run of fast code mostly measures noise.{/}

## The final closure form

{#C62828}Benchmark bodies written against the beta closure spelling need updating.{/}

{#0F766E}Part of the same sweep that deprecated the legacy closure decorators.{/}

## `create_task()`, or `create_raising_task()`

They run a coroutine on the AsyncRT runtime, producing a `Task` or a `RaisingTask`.

{#C62828}Experimental in 1.0.{/}

## A group of tasks that run concurrently

It is the structured unit for fanning work out and collecting it back.

{#C62828}Experimental in 1.0.{/} {#0F766E}`parallelism_level()` reports how much parallelism the runtime actually has.{/}

## No — the runtime is, the language feature is not

`std.runtime.asyncrt` ships and works, but the manual never introduces `async` as a language construct and first-class async remains unstarted on the roadmap.

{#C62828}Treat it as a library you opted into, not a language feature.{/}

## It may complete with an error

The error surfaces when the task's result is taken, so a failure inside a coroutine reaches the code that awaited it.

{#C62828}Experimental in 1.0.{/}

## `parallelism_level()`

It reports the runtime's parallelism level.

{#0F766E}Worth asking before sizing a fan-out: a task group wider than the runtime buys queueing, not speed.{/}

## A debugger, with editor integration

Breakpoints and stepping over Mojo source, rather than over the generated code.

{#0F766E}For compile-time evaluation there is nothing to step through — a `comptime` value either folds or reports why it cannot.{/}
